Introduction
The quantitative development of education in the last 20-25 years has been an element common to all countries in the world, the less developed as weil as the more developed. Education is nowadays universally recognized as a major factor affecting economic and social development and its numerical expansion has become one of the main concerns of present day policy-makers. However, the tremendous enrolment growth which has taken place since the Second World War has not been sufficient to prevent the existence of large disparities in the provision of education, not only between developed and developing countries but also between and within the latter countries. Furthermore, the problem of growing disparities allied to inefficiency, low quality, increasing costs and unemployment among schon leavers has recently put the school system in question though offen without suggesting suitable alternatives. Whatever the value of the above criticisms, it is nonetheless true that many countries have been experiencing quite different patterns of enrolment increases determined, by definition, not only by the relative size of their school-age populations but also by social, economic and political factors, raising sometimes major issues due either to the resulting financial burden or similar constraints. The following summary review is intended to highlight the salient quantitative patterns of the development of education during the period 1960 until 1976, i.e. the period for which comparable and comprehensive data are presently available in the Unesco Office of Statistics. To simplify the presentation, world features (excluding China and the Democratic People's Republic of Korea, for which time series are not available) will be grouped into two major areas - developed and developing countries. Comments and data will further be presented for Northern America, and the USSR as major regions in the former areas and Africa, Asia and Latin America for the latter. (Japan is included in Asia as well as in the group of developed countries). We will (1) review the trends and prospects of the school-age population, and (2) outline trends in enrolment, teaching staff and expenditure.
