The U.N. Population projections until 2000 allow an examination of the implications of the decline of mortality and changes in fertility as regards the size of school-age population. Thus, taking as an example the age-group 5-14 it is interesting to note that in spite of a fall in the rate of growth for the developing countries as a whole, this group would, between 1960 and 2000, double in Asia, increase 2.7 times in Latin America and triple in Africa. For the developed countries, on the other hand, while the total population is expected to increase (Northern America, 50 %; Europe and the USSR, 33 %) the growth rate of the population of the 5-14 age-group was decreasing between 1960 and 1975 and is expected to increase between 1975 and the year 2000 as shown in figure 1 below. Thus, whereas developing countries will be faced with an increasing pressure for more places to enrol their growing number of children, developed countries must take account of the consequences of the increases and decreases in school-age population. These differences in future prospects for the population of primary school age have obvious implications for the future requirement for teachers and financial resources.
