2. Enrolment ratios. Enrolment ratios relate the number ofpupils enrolled in a given age-group or level to a total population of a specific age-group. They permit an assessment of educational participation although it should be noted that the enrolment data refer to registration and not to actual attendance. Gross enrolment ratios in this paper relate enrolment at the regional level in first, second and third levels of education to the population age-groups 6-11, 12-17 and 18-23 respectively, which are the age-groups generally assumed to correspond to the three levels of enrolment. As educational structures differ between countries and since under- and overaged pupils are enrolled at each level, these ratlos only give a crude measure of the coverage at each level. Because of these two factors they may sometimes exceed 100%, i.e. the size of the age-group being used as a yardstick. They do however show the potential capacity of the system to enro! the population of a given age-group in the absence of under- and over-aged pupils. Table 4 presents the set of gross enrolment ratios by level for the period under study, The following summary remarks may be made on the table:
