3. Enrolment disparities by sex. Table 6 presents the enrolment of girls expressed as a percentage of the total, by level, for 1960 and 1976. It can be seen that in the developed countries, participation ofgirls is close to boys at first and second levels of education. This was already so in 1960. Considerable progress took place at the third level between 1960 and 1976. As for the developing countries, the situation is far from being the same except in the case of Latin America where the pattern is very close at the first and second levels of education and serious progress has been made at the third level. In Africa and Asia although improvements have been made, the gap remains large at all levels and especially so at the second and third levels of education. In 1976, girls represented only one-third of second level enrolment and one-quarter of third level in Africa and the corresponding percentages for Asia were 37% and 32% respectively.
