4. Major issues. Areas of concern closely associated to the intake and transition problems reviewed above are several and we will here mention two which deserve special attention: (a) Intei~nal efficiency in primary education in developing countries; (b) Enrolment at the second and third levels of education by major types of education and fields of study.
(i) Efficiency and drop-out in primary education
The expansion of enrolment in developing countries is seriously affected by wastage factors, namely drop-out and repetition. The extent to which the flow of pupils through education systems is affected by these two factors appears often to be independent of the level of enrolment. This can be illustrated by the patterns of drop-out observed for two cohorts, at the regional level, starting in 1965 and 1970, as shown in Table 8. It should be recalled that as the figures in this table are weighted regional averages the level of drop-out for each region is heavily influenced by the situation in the most populous countries.
