Teaching Staff
The number of teaching staff in the world grew during the period at a faster rate than enrolment. Thus, the total of 12.7 million teachers at all levels of education virtually doubled to reach 25.3 million. Of the net increase, 5.4 million joined the first level, 5.3 million the second level and 1.9 million the third level. Trends and multipliers as shown in Table 10 indicate a higher relative growth in developing than in developed countries, at all levels of education. In spite of this, the former countries had in 1976 more than 58 % of the total enrolment with only 46% of the world's teaching staff. Table 11 below presents trends in pupil-teacher ratios for the three levels of education. These ratlos show at thefirst level of education: (a) Higher numbers of pupils per teacher in developing than in developed countries. There are also large differences between the ratios for each of the developing regions. It is striking to note that in 1976 Africa and, to a lesser extent, Asia had almost twice as many pupils per teacher as in the developed countries ; (b) A general decrease in the ratios over the period considered in all regions except Africa and Asia where values remained practically stable. At the second level ofeducation, the difference between the ratios is smaller between developed and developing countries with the latter countries generally having higher values. Furthermore, while the developed countries tend to reduce their ratios, Africa increased them rather considerably while
