Concluding Remarks
The primary purpose of this summary Statistical review has been to present major facts and features in the development of education since 1960. A major issue indicated above was the demographic factor. Population changes will in the future, as was the case in the past, continue to affect the ability of countries to respond to the growing demand for education in a world in constant process of modernisation. In this connection, educational planners will need to develop alternative approaches and methodologies in order to better evaluate present realities and efficient strategies for the
[381


DEVELOPMENT OF EDUCATION IN THE WORLD 165
future. This is definitely true for both the developed and the developing countries. The significant expansion in enrolment implied a rise of 77% of total enrolment at the first, second and third levels of education between 1960 and 1976. The rise was of 32% in the developed and of 133% in the developing countries. However, disparities between developed and developing countries continued to be marked. Thus, developed countries, besides accomplishing practically universal primary and secondary education, experienced an exceptional boom at the third level. This is particularly so in Northern America. As for developing countries, despite a sharp increase of enrolment at the first level of education, late entrants and repeaters inflate their enrolment ratios, partly masking the increasing number of out-of-school population in a large number of countries. Furthermore, drop-out and repetition continue to be serious bottlenecks to the efficient utilization of available resources. Expansion has been noteworthy at the second level in Asia and at the third level in Latin America. They, however, remain proportionately moderate as is shown by their school pyramids. Although the percentage of total enrolment constituted by girls increased at all levels in all regions, the gap between the two sexes measured in absolute terms increased in several cases, namely at the third level in the developed countries, at all levels of education in Africa and Asia but only at the third level of education in Lätin America. In 1976, the developing countries had a higher percentage of their second level enrolment in General Education and a lower part in vocational and technical education thän did the developed countries. This is contrary to the situation in higher education where the share of scientific and technological fields in total enrolment did not differ much between regions. The number of teaching stall in the world grew at a faster rate than enrolment, the rate of growth being higher in developing than in developed countries, at all levels of education. Developing countries however, had in 1976 more than 58% of the total enrolment with only 46% of the world's teaching staff. The major difference in provision of teaching staff between developed and developing countries is found at the first level where there are higher numbers of pupils per teacher in the latter region. Public expenditure on education in the world, at current market prices, grew from 51 billion U.S. dollars in 1960 to 366 billion in 1976. There is no doubt that education remained one of the prioritary sectors for public concern, since the average annual growth rate of educational expenditure for the period was higher than that of the Gross National Product (13.1% as
[391


166 G. CARCELES
compared to 10.1%). This was the case for the developed countries (12.7% versus 9.9%) and even more so far the developing countries (16% versus 10.9%). As a result in 1976, public expenditure on education represented 5.8% of the Gross National Product for the world, compared with 3.8% in 1960. The figure for the developed countries was 6% in 1976 and 4% in 1960 compared to 4.8% and 2.3% respectively in the developing countries. Furthermore, the relatively high level of per pupil public expenditure as compared with per capita income in most African and Asian countries as well as in several Latin American countries, appears to be a serious constraint to potential expansion of education in these regions. In conclusion, social demand - heavily influenced in the developing countries by population pressures- will continue to place emphasis on educational needs. Extension of basic education and literacy to disadvantaged groups and areas will no doubt become a priority as will the reconsideration of educational content to match development needs. Out-ofschool and adult education will have to be taken more into account without, however, placing too many expectations upon their capacity to provide learning at lower costs than formal education. Finally it should be further realized that sound and pragmatic educational planning is only possible when based upon a solid, consistent and comprehensive statistical data base.
